WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.730
The phrases "Pixar movie" and "Easter eggs"
go together like peanut butter and jelly.

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:09.830
Fans always anticipate the studio's freshest
release to be full to bursting with fun references

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:14.150
and winks to pop culture, and director Lee
Unkrich's journey into the Land of the Dead

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:16.680
with Coco is by no means an exception.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.090
Here are just a few of the Easter eggs in
Coco you may have missed.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:25.520
And it goes without saying, but spoilers ahead.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:26.980
Famous food delivery

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:31.800
A Pixar movie must, the Pizza Planet delivery
truck first appeared in 1995's Toy Story,

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.910
and has popped up in almost every single film
the animation studio has rolled out since.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:41.320
The truck sits outside a trailer in A Bug's
Life, drives across a bridge in Ratatouille

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.309
— and it can be spotted in Coco right outside
Miguel's house near the beginning of the movie.

00:00:45.309 --> 00:00:50.980
As the young boy stares wistfully outside,
the Pizza Planet truck zooms past his window.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.260
The anticipated A113

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:57.690
Like the Pizza Planet truck, the letter-number
combination of "A113" has deep roots in the

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:02.739
Pixar film pantheon, though it extends its
reach into plenty of non-Pixar animated films

00:01:02.739 --> 00:01:04.030
and television shows.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:08.940
The four-figure code is actually a reference
to Room A113, the classroom that animation

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:12.800
industry A-listers attended at California
Institute of the Arts.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:17.900
And just like in every other Pixar movie,
A113 shows up in Coco, specifically in the

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:21.970
Land of the Dead, written backwards on the
door of the Bureau of Family Grievances, where

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:26.090
the deceased members of the Rivera family
go to coordinate a way to send Miguel back

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:27.720
where he belongs.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.160
Pixar Piñatas

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:33.770
Sticking with the nods to Pixar projects past,
Coco also features a tucked-away reference

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:37.090
to a few of its other movies: Toy Story and
Monsters, Inc.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:41.180
As Miguel dashes through his village's market
toward the central plaza in the beginning

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:45.220
of the movie, viewers will notice that he
runs past a bundle of piñatas in the shape

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:50.120
of cowboy Woody and astronaut Buzz from Toy
Story, and the one-eyed, green-skinned monster

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:51.880
Mike Wazowski from Monsters, Inc.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:56.700
Near the back of the piñata pack is a turquoise
one, likely designed to look like Mike's partner

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.300
Sully.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:01:59.370
Familiar figurines

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:03.980
After grabbing a bite to eat on his way to
the plaza, Miguel happens upon a table lined

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:07.600
with alebrijes [ah-lay-BREE-hayz], Mexican
folk art sculptures that depict fantastical

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.489
creatures painted in bright colors and bold
patterns.

00:02:10.489 --> 00:02:15.010
Hidden amongst these striking sculptures are
small figurines of the clownfish Marlin and

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:18.430
Nemo and the regal blue tang fish Dory from
Finding Nemo.

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:22.719
Look carefully and you'll also see trinkets
of the rat chef Remy from Ratatouille and

00:02:22.719 --> 00:02:25.829
the whale shark Destiny from Finding Dory.

00:02:25.829 --> 00:02:27.599
Never-forgotten Nemo

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:32.200
There's yet another Nemo figure for viewers
to find in Coco, this time on the Rivera family's

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:33.200
ofrenda.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.310
You'll spot the little guy during the scene
in which Abuelita Elena places the photo of

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:41.219
Mama Coco, Mama Imelda, and Miguel's great-great-grandfather.

00:02:41.219 --> 00:02:46.049
He sits atop a jar on the righthand side of
the screen, just behind a mustachioed skeleton

00:02:46.049 --> 00:02:47.469
in a suit and top hat.

00:02:47.469 --> 00:02:48.670
The Luxo Ball

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:54.150
Pixar's famous ball, officially called the
Luxo Ball in reference to the studio's first-ever

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:58.659
animated short Luxo Jr., is yet another staple
of the studio's efforts.

00:02:58.659 --> 00:03:03.189
The yellow bouncy ball, adorned with a thick
blue stripe and red star, appears in Coco

00:03:03.189 --> 00:03:06.159
during the rehearsal sequence featuring Frida
Kahlo.

00:03:06.159 --> 00:03:10.689
Watch the background carefully as Dante the
dog and Frida's monkey run after each other

00:03:10.689 --> 00:03:11.719
in the workshop.

00:03:11.719 --> 00:03:14.980
The ball can be seen on a table in a super
quick moment.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:16.739
The inspired performer

00:03:16.739 --> 00:03:21.049
If wardrobe choices are any indication, it's
possible that one of Pixar's oldest characters,

00:03:21.049 --> 00:03:25.439
Sid from the original Toy Story, may no longer
be in the Land of the Living.

00:03:25.439 --> 00:03:28.930
During the talent show, one performer took
the stage with a familiar looking t-shirt.

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:33.790
Sure, the hair's different, and he's not making
monsters out of toys, but only one guy in

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:36.620
Pixar's history was dark enough to wear that
shirt.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:41.680
Despite how sad it is that Sid's somehow ended
up in the Mexican afterlife, being a skeleton-DJ's

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.180
got to be better than being a garbage man.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:45.629
A legendary voice

00:03:45.629 --> 00:03:50.329
John Ratzenberger, an actor best known for
his long-running role on Cheers and his voiceover

00:03:50.329 --> 00:03:52.510
work, has become a Pixar legend.

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:56.700
He's lent his pipes to a character in every
one of the studio's films, including Hamm

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:02.260
the Piggy Bank in Toy Story, Mack the 18-Wheeler
in Cars, and the Yeti in Monsters, Inc.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:05.340
"Welcome to the Himalayas!"

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:11.579
As expected, Ratzenberger has a part in Coco,
but it's so brief that it likely breezed right

00:04:11.579 --> 00:04:12.579
by you.

00:04:12.579 --> 00:04:16.549
Ratzenberger voices a goofy skeleton whose
dentist is the one who remembers him in the

00:04:16.549 --> 00:04:19.949
Land of the Living, thanks to his massive
mouth of metal.

00:04:19.949 --> 00:04:22.509
Pixar's personal ofrenda

00:04:22.509 --> 00:04:27.190
Last but in no way least is a touching tribute
to a few influential people who have sadly

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:28.330
passed away.

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:32.800
The Coco team included its very own ofrenda
of sorts in the film's credits, with a series

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.430
of photographs honoring those who've had an
impact on Pixar as a whole.

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:42.020
Steve Jobs, the co-founder of Apple who was
also a CEO and shareholder of Pixar; Don Rickles,

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:46.930
famed insult comic and the voice of Mr. Potato
Head in the Toy Story movies; and Joe Ranft,

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:51.259
the voice actor behind Heimlich in A Bug's
Life and Jacques in Finding Nemo.

00:04:51.259 --> 00:04:55.629
Director Lee Unkrich confirmed Jobs and Rickles'
inclusion to Metro in an interview:

00:04:55.629 --> 00:05:00.080
"At the very end of the movie we kind of do
a digital ofrenda, where we gave everyone

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:03.919
in the company the opportunity to submit a
photograph of someone in their life who is

00:05:03.919 --> 00:05:06.780
no longer with them but had been supportive
of them.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:10.620
And we just filled the screen with hundreds
and hundreds of these beautiful images.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:14.470
We included people in that group that we had
lost from the Pixar family, people like Steve

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:16.639
Jobs and Don Rickles."

00:05:16.639 --> 00:05:17.789
Thanks for watching!

00:05:17.789 --> 00:05:20.569
